<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theuderic II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">587–<a href="/years/613">613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Theuderic II of Burgundy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theuderic II was the Merovingian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 595 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> from 612 until his death in 613, a son of Childebert II. Much of his reign was dominated by his grandmother Brunhild, and he fought his brother Theudebert II, whom he defeated and had killed in 612. He expelled the Irish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> from his kingdom around 610.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theuderic-ii.jpg" alt="Theuderic II" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sincona.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremissis_-_Theuderic_II_-_Avenches.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theuderic II was the second son of Childebert II. At his father's death in 595 he received the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> that had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a>'s, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, along with lordship over the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>, Blois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>. His elder brother Theudebert II received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. During his minority, and later, he reigned under the guidance of his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a>, whom Theudebert had evicted from Austrasia in 599. A peasant found her wandering near Arcis in Champagne and brought her to Theuderic, who welcomed her and readily fell under her influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a>'s influence inclined Theuderic toward war with Theudebert, and he defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>. Their cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Clotaire II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> then prompted the brothers to ally against him, and in 600 they defeated Clotaire at Dormelles. They divided the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a>, Theuderic receiving the territory between the Seine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, and campaigned together in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, where they installed Genialis as duke. The brothers then fought each other, and Theuderic defeated Theudebert at Étampes.</p><p>In 604 Clotaire invaded Theuderic's kingdom. Theuderic won at Étampes on the Louet, though his mayor of the palace, Berthoald, was killed, and the next mayor, Protadius, a partisan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a>, was assassinated by nobles when he urged war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>. In 610 Theuderic lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, the Saintois, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> and Champagne to his brother, and his men east of the Jura were defeated by the Alemanni. He routed Theudebert at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> about 611 and at Tolbiac in 612, captured him and gave him over to Brunhilda, who had him put in a monastery and probably had him murdered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theuderic married Ermenberga, daughter of the Visigothic king Witteric, at Chalon in 606 but sent her home in disgrace the next year. He had four sons by unnamed mistresses. He died of dysentery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in late 613, while preparing a campaign against Clotaire, who had retaken the duchy of Dentelin under a treaty made during the last war between the brothers. His son Sigebert II succeeded him in both kingdoms under the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theuderic II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Irish monk Columbanus founded Luxeuil there, but was expelled by King <b>Theuderic II</b> and Brunhild.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,6.18&amp;year=603" aria-label="Metz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Theuderic II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,6.18&amp;year=603">What was here in 603 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
